  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure, moving across southern Great Lakes\ninto southeastern Canada Thursday, will drag a strong front \nthrough the region late week. Showers will be followed by wind \nand cold.\n\nLow pressure will move from the central Plains into the southern\nGreat Lakes Thursday, then into southeastern Canada Thursday\nnight, before pushing into southeastern Canada Friday. A midweek\ncold front that could bring a few showers or a rumble of \nthunder to parts of the region will stall before pushing northward\nas a warm front and connect to the aforementioned low pressure \nsystem that will move into southeast Canada. As both the warm \nfront and low pressure push north and northeastward mid- late \nweek, a strong cold front extending from the low will push \nrapidly through the region. Noticeably colder air will push in \nbehind the cold front and spread throughout the region Friday \nnight into Saturday. Increased chances of precipitation will \naccompany this complex system. The warm front may bring higher \nrain chances along the Mason-Dixon and northern areas. The low \nwill stay to our north; however, the strong cold front will \nbring another round of showers and perhaps some thunder to any \nparts of our region late in the week. There may be enough cold \nair and moisture with cold frontal passage that rain showers may\nmix with snow showers at the highest elevations of western \nMaryland and northeastern West Virginia Friday night into early \nSaturday. Little to no snow accumulations expected at this time.\nHigh pressure will bring dry and cool conditions the remainder \nof the day Saturday and through Sunday. Northwest winds could \ngust 20 to 30 mph with as much as 40 mph in higher elevations \nbehind the strong cold front. Wind headlines may be needed late \nweek into the early half of the weekend.\n\nIn terms of temperatures, most areas from the Mason-Dixon and\nsouth to just north of US 33 could feel the affects of the\nmidweek cold front and have temperatures near average on\nThursday. Areas into the Virginia Piedmont and central \nShenandoah Valley could have above average temperatures as they \nwill be south of the front. Temperatures will be primarily in \nthe middle to upper 60s. All areas should feel the affect of a\nsurging warm front where temperatures warm up into middle to\nupper 70s on Friday. This, of course, will be right before the\nstrong cold front plunges southward and drops our temperatures\nfrom the middle 70s to the middle 40s over a 12 hour period into\nearly Saturday morning. Highs on Saturday could struggle to\nreach 60 degrees anywhere. As a matter of fact, highs in the\nmountains could only reach the upper 30s to middle 40s. Sunday\nmay only be a few degrees milder with highs reaching the upper\n50s in the west and north and near 60 elsewhere.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Relatively dry & very warm conditions to begin \nthe work week before chances for showers and a thunderstorms \nreturn by Wednesday evening.\n\nAs remnants from Isaias fully clear out of our region today, mid-\nlevel ridging builds over the region through the middle of the week.\nHigh pressure and southwesterly surface winds lead to mostly dry and \nsunny conditions Tuesday and Wednesday morning. Daytime temperatures \nraise into the low to mid 80s both days, well above average for mid-\nOctober. \n\nWednesday afternoon, a surface high over the Dakotas slides westward \nover the Great Lakes, nudging a low pressure system over the Ohio \nRiver Valley south into Kentucky and West Virginia. Northwesterly \nwinds off of this high slowly advances a cold front southwards \nbefore stalling out in Virginia. A few showers and thunderstorms \ncould form Wednesday afternoon as the warm front associated with the \nlow over the Tennessee Valley slides northward into our area. With \nthis stalled front lingering in the area, showers and thunderstorms \nremain possible across our forecast area through Thursday evening. \nThe best chances for showers and thunderstorms lies west of the Blue \nRidge.",
